Architecture
============

Scope
-----

One Node process serves three things on a single port:

1. A JSON REST API mounted at /api.
2. A Socket.IO server on the same HTTP server, used for push-free live
   refresh in both clients.
3. Startup schema sync and seeding against PostgreSQL.

Two clients consume it: the Expo mobile app (students) and the Vite admin
console (Campus Recreation staff). There is no server-rendered UI.


Request path
------------

	HTTP request
	  -> cors (CLIENT_ORIGINS allowlist)
	  -> express.json
	  -> /api router (src/routes/index.ts)
	  -> feature router (src/routes/xRoutes.ts)
	  -> validate(schema)        optional, zod
	  -> authenticate            optional, verifies Bearer access token
	  -> authorize("admin")      optional, role gate
	  -> controller (asyncHandler wrapped)
	  -> models / services
	  -> serializer
	  -> res.json
	  -> errorHandler on any throw

Nothing bypasses this order. If a controller needs a user id it reads
req.user.userId, which authenticate populates from the JWT payload.


Layers and what belongs in each
-------------------------------

src/routes/
	URL to handler mapping and middleware order. No logic. A route file
	should be readable as a table of contents for its feature.

src/controllers/
	One exported handler per endpoint. Owns HTTP concerns: reading params,
	starting transactions, choosing status codes, calling serializers,
	emitting socket events. Business rules that more than one controller
	needs do not live here.

src/services/
	Reusable domain logic with no knowledge of Request or Response.
	dailyQuests.ts resolves the active quest set for a date. services/badges/
	is the badge engine. emailService.ts and its templates send mail.
	passwordResetService.ts and emailVerificationService.ts own OTP lifecycles.

src/models/
	Sequelize class definitions and column types only. Relations are declared
	separately in models/associations.ts, which src/index.ts calls once at
	boot before sync.

src/utils/serializers.ts
	The contract layer. This file defines the exact JSON shape both frontends
	parse. Renaming a database column should require changes here and nowhere
	else. Adding a field to a client means adding it here, not in a controller.

src/realtime/io.ts
	Owns the Socket.IO instance. Controllers import { emit } and fire named
	events; they never touch the io object directly.

src/middleware/
	auth.ts (authenticate, authorize), validate.ts (zod), errorHandler.ts
	(single JSON error shape).


Authentication model
--------------------

* Access token: short-lived JWT, sent as Authorization: Bearer <token>.
  Signed with JWT_ACCESS_SECRET. Payload carries userId, role, name.
* Refresh token: long-lived, hashed into the refresh_tokens table. The raw
  token is only ever held by the client. POST /api/auth/refresh exchanges it
  for a new access token.
* Students authenticate at /api/auth/login. Staff authenticate at
  /api/admin/login. Both mint the same token pair; role separates them.
* The Socket.IO handshake reuses the access token. Same secret, same verify
  function, so an expired REST token means an expired socket too.

University single sign-on is not implemented. JWT is the authentication layer
for this phase and was designed so an SSO provider can be added in front of it
without touching controllers. See docs/maintenance.md.


Schema management
-----------------

src/index.ts calls sequelize.sync({ alter: true }) at every boot. There is no
migration tool in the project. Consequences you must know about:

* Adding a nullable column is safe.
* Adding a NOT NULL column to a table that already has rows will fail at boot
  with a Postgres 23502 error. Either add it nullable and backfill, or clear
  the table in development.
* Column drops and type narrowing are not reliably applied by alter.
* Production deployment should switch to sequelize-cli migrations before the
  database holds real student data.

runSeed() in src/config/seed.ts runs after sync and is idempotent. It creates
the bootstrap admin from SEED_ADMIN_* and any baseline reference rows.


Adding a feature: the checklist
-------------------------------

1. Model in src/models/, relation in models/associations.ts if it joins.
2. Serializer function in src/utils/serializers.ts for anything the clients
   will read.
3. Controller in src/controllers/, wrapped in asyncHandler, transaction if it
   writes more than one table.
4. Route file, registered in src/routes/index.ts.
5. If a client should react without polling, emit a socket event and document
   it in docs/realtime.md.
6. Update docs/api.md.